---@class posthog.signals.CreateSignalsReportRequest
---@field project_id string Project ID of the project you're trying to access. To find the ID of the project, make a call to /api/projects/.
---@field title string One-line report title the inbox shows. Conventional-commit style (`type(scope): description`, e.g. `fix(insights): missing series color`) renders with type/scope styling.
---@field summary string The report body the inbox shows. Markdown is supported (headings, lists, code, links; images are not rendered). Lead with one plain declarative sentence — the inbox card uses your first line verbatim as the headline (~140 chars, emphasis stripped), then renders the full markdown in the detail view. A heading, or a bold label on a line of its own with a blank line above it, marks a section that a threaded Slack delivery splits into its own reply.
---@field evidence posthog.signals.CreateSignalsReportRequestEvidenceList The observations backing the report — each becomes a bound signal. At least one.
---@field actionability_explanation string 2-3 sentence evidence-grounded justification for the actionability call below.
---@field actionability posthog.signals.ActionabilityEnum|string The scout's actionability call: `immediately_actionable` -> the report surfaces READY; `requires_human_input` -> PENDING_INPUT; `not_actionable` -> suppressed. A safety-judge failure suppresses the report regardless. A root cause you have not found is not human input: a report that names the evidence, the code surface, or a reproducible failure path is `immediately_actionable`, because investigating it is the action. Reserve `requires_human_input` for a report blocked on a decision only a person can make. * `immediately_actionable` - immediately_actionable * `requires_human_input` - requires_human_input * `not_actionable` - not_actionable
---@field already_addressed? boolean Whether the issue is already being handled — fixed in recent changes, or with a fix in flight (an open PR, a recently active branch, an assigned / in-progress issue or agent task). Gates autostart of a scout report, so a wrong `false` opens a duplicate PR. Tracked separately.
---@field repository? string|distilled.Null Optional repo for opening a draft PR, by autostart of a scout report or by a person from the inbox. Pass `owner/repo` whenever you can say where a fix would land. Omit the field when you can't, which triggers free-form selection across the team's repos (the slow path on a many-repo team). Keep the `NO_REPO` sentinel for the rare report where nothing under version control could change, since a skill body, a config file, or a doc still lives in a repo.
---@field priority? posthog.signals.AutonomyPriorityEnum|string|distilled.Null Optional priority (`P0`-`P4`). Required for autostart; pair with `priority_explanation`. * `P0` - P0 * `P1` - P1 * `P2` - P2 * `P3` - P3 * `P4` - P4
---@field priority_explanation? string|distilled.Null 2-3 sentence justification for `priority`. Required when `priority` is set.
---@field suggested_reviewers? posthog.signals.CreateSignalsReportRequestSuggestedReviewersList Optional reviewers to route the report to (each a `github_login` and/or `user_uuid`). This is the primary way a report reaches a human — the inbox floats a reviewer's own reports to the top of their inbox even when no PR is involved — so set it whenever you can name a plausible owner. On a scout report it also gates autostart: a PR opens only if at least one reviewer clears their autonomy threshold. A report a person files never autostarts.
---@field charts? posthog.signals.CreateSignalsReportRequestChartsList Optional charts to attach to the report — the inbox renders them inline, so a metric move is something the reader sees rather than a number they take on trust. Attach one whenever the finding rests on a trend, a spike, or a comparison you already queried. Reference a chart from `summary` as `[label](chart:<chart_id>)` to place it in the body; an unreferenced chart renders below the summary.
---@field metrics? posthog.signals.CreateSignalsReportRequestMetricsList Optional typed impact measurements, at most 6. Mark one metric `primary` for the key observation and the rest `supporting`, for users, sessions, occurrences, conversion, latency, or revenue. Every metric needs a live `query`: an InsightVizNode wrapping one bounded TrendsQuery built only from EventsNode or ActionsNode sources, with at most 1000 estimated longitudinal points. An affected-users metric counts distinct people through one series with `math: dau`; never sum its buckets. A `value`/`value_at` snapshot is only an optional cached fallback, and a metric without a query is invalid.
---@field suggested_prompts? posthog.signals.CreateSignalsReportRequestSuggestedPromptsList Optional follow-up prompts to offer above the report's `Ask AI` box: questions to ask, or next-step actions to request (e.g. carrying out the report's recommendation). The reader clicks one to fill the box with it, then sends or edits it. Write the prompts your own research left open, phrased as the reader would send them.
---@field links? posthog.signals.CreateSignalsReportRequestLinksList Typed, directed links from the new report to reports that already exist. Send them here, not in a later `edit-report` call, because autostart reads them when the report is created: a `duplicate_of` link to a report that already has a pull request, or a `depends_on` link to a report with no pull request yet, stops a second draft PR. Only the new report gets a row, so link from the side the sentence starts at. Links of the same kind must stay acyclic and every report must be in this project.
---@field source_issues? posthog.signals.CreateSignalsReportRequestSourceIssuesList The GitHub issues this report is about, when the finding came from an issue tracker. Pass the issue whenever the report is about one, so the report links back to it: a GitHub issue signal for the same issue then joins this report instead of opening a second one. When the project turned on GitHub issue comments, the issue gets one comment that links the report once its GitHub source ingests the issue. At most 5 per call.
---@field tags? posthog.signals.CreateSignalsReportRequestTagsList Optional slug tags that group the report with related ones, at most 10. Write lowercase kebab-case slugs that name the category of the report (`cost-spike`, `tracking-gap`), not the specific entity. Reuse a slug the project already uses before you coin a new one. Near-miss formats are normalized to slugs, and a tag that is empty or longer than 50 characters after normalization is rejected.
---@field idempotency_key? string|distilled.Null Optional name for this emission, unique within a scout's run or within a person's own reports. Reuse it verbatim to retry a call whose outcome you don't know (a timeout, a dropped connection): the retry returns the report the first call authored, with `idempotent_replay` true, instead of a second report. Omit it and the report's own content is the key, which covers a retry of the identical call — pass one when a retry might reword the report.

---Optional charts to attach to the report — the inbox renders them inline, so a metric move is something the reader sees rather than a number they take on trust. Attach one whenever the finding rests on a trend, a spike, or a comparison you already queried. Reference a chart from `summary` as `[label](chart:<chart_id>)` to place it in the body; an unreferenced chart renders below the summary.
---@alias posthog.signals.CreateSignalsReportRequestChartsList posthog.signals.ReportChart[]

