---One row in `inventory.top_events`.
---@class posthog.signals.TopEventEntry
---@field window_days number Rolling lookback window (in days) that every count and timestamp on this row is measured over — these are windowed figures, NOT lifetime totals. A capture gap can collapse a real, high-volume project's in-window counts to near-zero, so a thin `count` here does not by itself mean the project is low-volume: rule out an ingestion gap (compare against a trailing baseline via a direct `execute-sql`) before closing out a surface as unused.
---@field event string Event name as captured.
---@field count number Number of occurrences within the last `window_days` (windowed, not lifetime).
---@field distinct_users number `uniq(person_id)` over the window — reach. Distinguishes a high-count event firing on one power user from one firing on many users.
---@field recent_24h_count number Count in just the last 24 hours. Compare to `count / window_days` to spot bursts: a ratio well above `1 / window_days` means the event is concentrated in the last day.
---@field recent_24h_users number `uniq(person_id)` over just the last 24 hours. A burst across many users is qualitatively different from one user in a loop.
---@field first_seen_in_window string|distilled.Null ISO-8601 timestamp of the earliest occurrence within the `window_days` window. Compare to the window start to spot new event types: close to `now` ⇒ likely new or recently bursting; close to the window edge ⇒ has been around at least that long (the window can't tell you when the event *truly* first appeared).
---@field last_seen_in_window string|distilled.Null ISO-8601 timestamp of the most recent occurrence within the `window_days` window.

